          "where": "sentence:1",
          "text": "A community center kept a freezer for donated food. Part of the food was for families who needed help that week, and part was emergency stock for storms. In August, volunteers noticed that several meals had passed their best-use dates while other shelves became almost empty. A group of students studied the freezer log instead of simply asking for more donations. The log recorded the date, food type, number of packs, and whether each pack was marked ‘weekly' or ‘emergency.' The students found two patterns. First, new weekly donations were often placed in front of older ones, so volunteers took the newest packs first. Second, emergency stock was checked only once a month, which made it easy to miss items that were close to their dates. The students suggested three changes. Older weekly packs would be placed in a clearly marked front basket. Emergency food would still be protected, but volunteers would check its dates every week and replace near-date packs with newer ones. Finally, the center would write the number of available weekly packs on a small board before accepting extra donations of the same kind. After six weeks, the freezer still held the required emergency amount, but fewer meals were thrown away. The students did not reduce the safety stock. They used the same records more carefully to balance immediate needs and future emergencies. The center kept the old procedure in the file too, so future volunteers could compare the results and understand why the new system had been adopted.",
          "match": "made it easy"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "textbook": "ニューホライズン",
      "grade": "3",
      "section": "Unit 6-4",
      "feature": "ENOUGH_TO",
      "occurrences": 1,
      "evidenceStatus": "NO_EVIDENCE_BOUNDARY",
      "samples": [
        {
          "where": "sentence:1",
          "text": "Class 3-2 was planning a museum visit for forty students and four adults. The museum required school groups to reserve entry times for three popular rooms. The class wanted to see all three before lunch, but the first plan did not fit the rules. Instead of guessing, the planning team put the capacity table, timed-entry card, and floor guide next to one another. The capacity table listed Local Life Room: 24 people, Industry Room: 30 people, and Special Gallery: 20 people. The timed-entry card offered starting times at 10:00, 10:40, and 11:20. A group needed about thirty minutes in each room, and the museum allowed no more than two school groups to begin at the same time. The floor guide showed that Local Life and Industry were on the first floor. The Special Gallery was upstairs, and walking there from either first-floor room took about seven minutes. The class lunch reservation was at 12:20 in a hall beside the entrance. Everyone needed to be back there by 12:10. The first draft divided the class into two groups of twenty-two. At 10:00, Group A would enter Local Life and Group B would enter Industry. At 10:40, both groups would exchange rooms. At 11:20, they would both enter the Special Gallery. That final step was impossible because forty-four people could not enter a room with capacity twenty. The team considered making three groups, but the timed-entry rule created another problem. Three groups could not all start a reserved room at 10:00. So the students kept two groups but changed the order. Group A, twenty-two people, used Local Life at 10:00 and Industry at 10:40. Group B, also twenty-two, used Industry at 10:00, walked upstairs, and entered the Special Gallery at 10:40. At 11:20, Group A split temporarily: eighteen students and two adults entered the Special Gallery, while the remaining four students and two adults visited a free exhibit nearby. Group B used Local Life at 11:20. The small group from A could visit the free exhibit first and then join the others after the reserved room. The revised plan was not perfectly equal, but it satisfied every capacity and starting-time rule. It also left enough time to return downstairs and reach lunch by 12:10. The students wrote one note beside the plan: room order may differ, but every student will see the same three main exhibits. By linking the three materials, the team found a workable schedule without pretending that one simple route could fit all forty-four visitors.",
          "match": "enough time to return"
        }
      ]
    },
