        {
          "where": "sentence:1",
          "text": "A number about the town appeared again and again in posters, speeches, and social media: ‘Eighty percent of local teenagers volunteer every month.' A newspaper club wanted to use the number in an article about community service. Before printing it, Riku tried to find the original source. Several websites repeated the same sentence but did not explain where it came from. He finally found a city report from two years earlier. The report did contain the number 80 percent, but the survey had been given only to members of school volunteer clubs. The question also asked whether they had joined at least one activity during the previous three months, not whether they volunteered every month. The famous sentence had changed both the group and the time period. The club did not conclude that teenagers were uninterested in volunteering. The report could not answer that larger question. In the article, they wrote that 80 percent of surveyed volunteer-club members had participated at least once in three months. They also explained why this was different from saying 80 percent of all local teenagers volunteered monthly. Tracing the number to its source made the statement less dramatic but much more accurate.",
          "match": "whether they "
        }
      ]
    }
